# Side-by-side comparison

When a screen asks a person to judge whether one value is close enough to
another, show the two values touching. Two text values or two separate swatches
can be read by a machine. A person needs to see the values next to each other
to spot a small difference.

## When it applies

Look for this whenever a feature shows two versions of the same value:

- App value compared with the design data value (Migration analysis)
- Saved compared with working, or before compared with after (Changes, reset
  previews, Undo)
- Local compared with upstream, or old version compared with new version
- Current compared with proposed or generated (rules, scales, recipes,
  migration mapping)
- One mode compared with another (Light and Dark, or a default and an
  override)

If the comparison is what the feature is for, build it in. Otherwise, suggest
it in your plan or summary and ask before you add it. Working rule 5 still
applies.

## How it looks

- Put the two values in one cell or block, in the same order as the text
  columns or labels nearby. The "from" or app value goes first and the "to" or
  design data value goes second.
- The two halves are the same size, with no gap, border, outline, padding, or
  rounding between them. A border on the outside is fine.
- Keep the text values and result badges. The visual comparison is extra
  evidence, not a replacement.
- Colors: fill each half with the resolved color.
- Opacity: draw each half over the checkerboard with `transparencyBackground`
  and the authored surface from `elementsSurfaceStyleAtom`.
- Other value types (dimensions, radius, type styles, shadows): use the same
  shape for both halves so only the value changes. For example, two bars that
  start at the same edge, or two text samples at the same baseline.
- If either value can't be resolved, show "Unavailable". Never invent a value.
- Accessibility: give the pair `role="img"` and an `aria-label` that names
  both sides, such as "App value beside design data value". Color is never the
  only signal; the text values and badges carry the meaning.

## Reference implementation

`AdjacentComparison` in `src/views/analysis/ColorOpacityTable.tsx`, styled by
`.analysisAdjacentComparison` in `src/views/analysis/AnalysisView.css`.
`comparisonPaint` in `colorOpacityPresentation.ts` decides what each half
shows. When a second screen needs the pattern, move it into
`src/components/` and add it to the `component-catalog` skill instead of
copying it.

## Testing

Assert that the right edge of the first half equals the left edge of the
second half, that both borders are `0px`, and that a known non-matching pair
shows different colors. See `tests/e2e/color-opacity-coverage.spec.ts`.
